than expected: </span>Mic on calls</li><li class="e_9i e_bj"><span class="e_-i">✕</span><span class="e_Dj">Worse than expected: </span>Fit and build after months</li></ul><div class="e_lib e_qj"><span id=":R3namke67r6d6:" class="e_mib">Did this help you decide?</span><span class="e_nib" role="group" aria-labelledby=":R3namke67r6d6:"><button type="button" class="e_oib">Yes</button><button type="button" class="e_oib">No</button></span><span class="e_rib">1 of 1 found this helpful</span><div class="e_vib"></div><button type="button" class="e_5hb">Ask nimble_birch_87</button></div><div id=":Rnamke67r6d6:" class="e_9hb e_Vj" hidden=""><ul class="e_-hb"><li><button type="button" class="e_6hb">Still using them every day?</button></li><li><button type="button" class="e_6hb">Comfortable with glasses on?</button></li><li><button type="button" class="e_6hb">How well does the noise cancelling handle a plane or a train?</button></li></ul><div class="e_jib"></div></div></div></article></div></section></div></div></div></div><div></div><div class="e_e e_lf"> <p><strong><a href="https://www.soundguys.com/sony-ult-wear-review-113412/">Sony ULT WEAR</a>:</strong> These headphones fall outside our $150 MSRP limit, but they’re worth considering when discounted below $150. We measured 84% average noise reduction, and they lasted 32 hours and 35 minutes in our battery test. They’re comfortable and offer plenty of features, although their excessive bass strength won’t appeal to everyone.</p> <hr/> <p><strong><a href="https://www.soundguys.com/nothing-headphone-a-review-154232/">Nothing Headphone (a)</a>:</strong> Another option to watch for sales below $150. The Headphone (a) earned an 83% ANC score in our testing and offer distinctive physical controls, LDAC support, and extensive app features. Their sound needs some EQ adjustment, however, and their regular price puts them well above our budget ceiling.</p> <hr/> </div><div class="e_Zrb e_g5 e_h5 e_e"><a id="deal-9" class="e_k">Anker Soundcore Space One</a><div class="e__rb"></div><div class="e_-rb"></div><a href="https://dealhunt.co/us-B0C6KJ3R71" class="e_1rb">Anker Soundcore Space One</a><div class="e_2rb">Comfortable fit • Easy controls • Soundcore app</div><div class="e_3rb"><div class="e_zO e_8rb"><div class="e_AO"></div></div><span class="e_9rb">MSRP: $74.99</span><div class="e_WE e_7rb e_YE"></div></div><div class="e_4rb">Luxury features at a budget price.</div><div class="e_5rb">If you are in the market for noise canceling headphones, the Anker Soundcore Space One is worth considering. These headphones bring most of the same features of flagship competitors for a fraction of the price.</div><div class="e_6rb"><div class="e_1pb e_2pb"><div class="e_5pb"><a href="https://dealhunt.co/us-B0C6KJ3R71" class="e_Q9 e_lcb e_3pb" title="$99.99 at Amazon"><div class="e_R9"><span class="e_S9">$99.99 at Amazon</span></div></a></div><div class="e_dqb"><div class="e_kqb e_Zwb"><div class="e_lqb"></div></div></div></div></div><div class="e_dsb"><div class="e_XJ e_CJ"><div class="e_aJ"><div class="e_hJ"><button type="button" class="e_Lcb e_Ocb" title="Overlay this card&#x27;s lab curves with your pair and up to two more cards"><span class="e_Qcb">Pin to compare</span><small class="e_Rcb">0 of 3</small></button><button type="button" class="e_iJ"><span>Measurements</span><small class="e_lJ"></small></button><button type="button" class="e_iJ" disabled=""><span>Owners</span><small class="e_lJ">none yet</small></button></div></div></div></div></div><div></div><div class="e_e e_lf"> <p><a href="https://www.soundguys.com/anker-soundcore-space-one-review-111648/"><strong>Anker Soundcore Space One</strong></a>: The predecessor to our Best Overall pick remains worth considering if you want to spend less. Our testing gave the Space One an 80% ANC score, and we measured nearly 43 hours of battery life with ANC enabled. They also offer LDAC, multipoint connectivity, and a customizable EQ. Their default sound has elevated bass and treble strength, but the app gives you ways to adjust it.</p> </div><hr class="e_e e_U6"/><span class="e_C6"><div class="e_D6" id="id-what-to-look-for-7142"></div></span><div class="e_e e_lf"> <h2>What to look for in good budget noise canceling headphones</h2> </div><a class="e_i6 e_f" href="https://www.soundguys.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/W830NB_14-scaled.jpeg/"><div class="e_wqb"><div class="e_qub e_xqb"><div class="e_8O">Harley Maranan / SoundGuys</div></div><div class="e_4Bb e_lf">The Edifier W830NB headphones offer a solid feature set for a sub $100 pair of cans.</div></div></a><div class="e_e e_lf"> <p>You don’t need to spend $300 to get effective noise canceling, but not all budget headphones deliver the same experience. We’ve tested plenty of affordable models that look impressive on paper, only to find that their ANC struggles with everyday noise, their controls are frustrating, or their battery life falls short of the manufacturer’s claims.</p> </div><div class="e_e e_lf"><q>Prioritize effective noise canceling, a comfortable fit, and independently tested battery life over impressive-looking specifications.</q></div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>The best budget noise canceling headphones balance effective ANC with comfort, sound quality, and practical features. We’d rather have headphones that consistently reduce the rumble of a bus and stay comfortable for a full workday than a pair loaded with features we rarely use.</p></div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p><strong>Here are the 10 things we’d pay attention to before buying:</strong></p> <ul> <li><strong>Effective active noise canceling:</strong> Look for independent ANC measurements rather than manufacturer claims about maximum noise reduction. Our testing shows that the Soundcore Space 2 reduce an average of 84% of outside noise, while the $79.99 JLab JBuds Lux ANC manage around 80%. ANC generally works best against consistent low-frequency sounds, such as engine rumble.</li> <li><strong>A comfortable fit:</strong> Pay attention to ear cushion depth, headband padding, weight, and clamping force. A good seal around your ears also helps ANC and isolation work effectively.</li> <li><strong>Battery life with ANC enabled:</strong> Manufacturers sometimes advertise their longest battery figures with ANC switched off. We test with ANC enabled so you can make meaningful comparisons. The 1MORE SonoFlow lasted 56 hours and 51 minutes in our testing, while the EarFun Wave Pro managed 52 hours and 31 minutes.</li> <li><strong>Sound quality you can adjust:</strong> A companion app with a useful EQ can help you adjust bass, midrange, and treble strength to your preferences. We particularly appreciate apps that let you save custom settings instead of limiting you to presets.</li> <li><strong>Bluetooth multipoint:</strong> If you regularly switch between a laptop and phone, multipoint connectivity saves you from repeatedly disconnecting and pairing your headphones. Check whether multipoint works alongside your preferred Bluetooth codec.</li> <li><strong>Wired listening options:</strong> A 3.5mm input or USB-C audio connection can be useful for airplanes and devices without Bluetooth. Don’t assume ANC works in wired mode, though, since some budget headphones disable noise canceling or EQ when connected by cable.</li> <li><strong>Portable design and accessories:</strong> Folding ear cups and an included carrying case make a difference if you travel frequently. The EarFun Wave Pro are particularly practical here, while the CMF Headphone Pro don’t fold.</li> <li><strong>Easy-to-use controls:</strong> Check how easily you can change volume, skip tracks, switch ANC modes, and activate transparency mode. Physical buttons can be easier to operate without looking.</li> <li><strong>Microphone performance</strong>: If you take calls in busy places, look for microphone tests recorded with background noise. Headphones can reduce noise effectively for the listener while still struggling to make your voice understandable to someone on a call.</li> <li><strong>Build quality and replaceable parts:</strong> Budget headphones often use plastic, which isn’t necessarily a problem. Look for sturdy hinges and headbands, along with replaceable ear cushions if you plan to keep your headphones for several years.</li> </ul> </div><hr class="e_e e_U6"/><span class="e_C6"><div class="e_D6" id="id-why-you-should-trust-us-7142"></div></span><div class="e_e e_lf"> <h2>Why you should trust us</h2> </div><div role="button" class="e_f"><div class="e_wqb"><div class="e_qub e_xqb"></div><div class="e_4Bb e_lf">CMF lets you mix and match earcups’ colors to your heart’s content.</div></div></div><div class="e_e e_lf"> <p>At SoundGuys, we’ve tested hundreds of headphones across every price range. One thing I’ve learned is that spending more doesn’t always mean getting a better experience. I’ve been impressed by affordable headphones that outperform expectations, and disappointed by expensive models that struggle with the basics. That’s why I focus on real-world performance rather than marketing claims.</p></div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>Our team uses standardized tests to measure <a href="https://www.soundguys.com/noise-canceling-anc-explained-28344/">ANC</a>, sound quality, battery life, and microphone performance. I use those results alongside our hands-on experience to judge comfort, controls, and everyday usability. After all, impressive specifications don’t mean much if headphones become uncomfortable after an hour.</p> </div><div class="e_e e_lf"><q>I&#x27;ve learned that the best budget headphones aren&#x27;t necessarily the ones with the most features, but the ones that get the essentials right.</q></div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>Battery life is a great example. The 1MORE SonoFlow lasted 56 hours and 51 minutes with ANC enabled in our testing, exceeding the manufacturer’s 50-hour claim. That’s why I distinguish between independently tested results and advertised figures.</p> </div><div class="e_e e__4"><div class="e_24 e_Aqb"><span class="e_Dqb">Readers&#x27; poll</span><h3 class="e_Eqb">What matters most to you when buying budget noise canceling headphones?</h3><div class="e_Fqb">16 votes</div><div class="e_Gqb e_f5"><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Effective noise canceling</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">50%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Sound quality</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">44%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Comfort and fit</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">0%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Long battery life</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">0%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Features and connectivity</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">0%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button><button type="button" class="e_Hqb"><div class="e_Jqb"><span>Price and overall value</span></div><div class="e_Cqb">6%</div><div class="e_Bqb"><div class="e_Nqb"></div></div></button></div></div></div><div class="e_e e_lf"> <p>For this guide, I’ve limited our main picks to headphones with an MSRP below $150. I’ve also considered the compromises, from missing carrying cases to awkward controls and microphone performance. My goal is to help you find headphones that fit your needs without paying for features you won’t use.</p> </div><hr class="e_e e_U6"/><div class="e_e e_lf"> <h2>Frequently asked questions about the best budget noise canceling headphones</h2> </div><div class="e_e e_X5"><button type="button" class="e_25"><span class="e_35">How can ANC headphones be so cheap?</span></button><div class="e_15"><div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>Noise canceling technology has become more affordable as manufacturers improve their designs and production processes. Budget headphones can now offer effective ANC without the premium materials or extra features found on more expensive models. Our testing shows that several headphones under $100 reduce around 80% of perceived outside noise.</p> </div></div></div></div><div class="e_e e_X5"><button type="button" class="e_25"><span class="e_35">Are cheap ANC headphones any good?</span></button><div class="e_15"><div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>Yes. The JLab JBuds Lux ANC cost $79.99 and reduced approximately 80% of outside noise in our testing. You may have to compromise on build quality, accessories, or certain features, but affordable headphones can still deliver effective ANC, comfortable designs, and useful battery life.</p> </div></div></div></div><div class="e_e e_X5"><button type="button" class="e_25"><span class="e_35">What happens if you wear noise canceling headphones all day?</span></button><div class="e_15"><div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>You can wear noise canceling headphones for extended periods, provided they’re comfortable and you keep the volume at a safe level. Take occasional breaks if you experience discomfort, and use transparency mode or remove your headphones when you need to stay aware of your surroundings.</p> </div></div></div></div><div class="e_e e_X5"><button type="button" class="e_25"><span class="e_35">Why can I still hear with noise canceling headphones?</span></button><div class="e_15"><div><div class="e_e e_lf"><p>ANC works best against steady, low-frequency sounds, such as engine rumble or ventilation noise. Voices, sudden sounds, and some higher-frequency noises can still get through. Even the best noise canceling headphones won’t eliminate every sound around you.</p> </div></div></div></div><div id="comments-section" class="e_pN"><section class="e_39" aria-label="Join the conversation"><div class="e_49"><div class="e_59"><span class="e_79">+</span></div><div class="e_89"><h2 class="e_99">Conversation</h2><p class="e_-9">Read what readers said or add your take.</p></div></div><button type="button" class="e_a-">Open conversation</button></section></div></div></div></div></main><footer class="e_JE"></footer><div class="e_62 e_8D e_-D e_72" role="presentation"></div><div class="e_aE"></div><div class="e_eH"><div class="e_fH"><div class="e_gH"><div class="e_iH"></div><div class="e_jH"></div></div></div></div></div></body></html>